    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d338">

                <pb facs="0341" n="271" sameAs="frus:frus1934v04/pg271"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">724.3415/3886: Telegram (part air)</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-06-28T16:00:00+01:00">June 28, 1934—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 29—7:18 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>186. Department’s 70, June 20, 11 a.m.<anchor n="98" xml:id="d338-f1"/> The
                    following is a paraphrase of the British reply: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Government propose to continue for the present to hold up the issuing of
                            licenses’ for the export of arms to these two countries but feel it
                            would be inadvisable for them to make a formal declaration on the lines
                            proposed in the Committee’s report until the governments of all the
                            countries included in the list of countries whose cooperation Great
                            Britain regards as essential (see Consulate’s 98, May 28 noon) have
                            announced that they are willing to participate without
                                reservations.”<anchor n="99" xml:id="d338-f2"/></p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d338-f1" n="98" xml:id="d338-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram No. 70 instructed the Consul to “telegraph substance of replies as
                    received.” (724.3415/3841)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d338-f2" n="99" xml:id="d338-f2-content">
                <p>For slightly different text, see League of Nations, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Official Journal</hi>, July 1934, p. 842.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
